1
00:00:01,218 --> 00:00:05,997
Hi, welcome to my presentation, From
Prompts to Review Harnesses Scaling AI

2
00:00:06,268 --> 00:00:08,938
Code Reviews Across 70+ Software Projects.

3
00:00:09,938 --> 00:00:10,518
Who am I?

4
00:00:10,938 --> 00:00:11,528
I'm Guido.

5
00:00:11,578 --> 00:00:13,638
I'm an executive consultant at CGI.

6
00:00:13,798 --> 00:00:17,877
I've been working in the industry
for 15 years or so, and have been

7
00:00:17,878 --> 00:00:23,047
with CGI for a few of those Now, what
are we going to talk about today?

8
00:00:23,897 --> 00:00:26,247
First of all, why did
we even do what we did?

9
00:00:26,747 --> 00:00:29,077
Then what was our initial approach?

10
00:00:29,407 --> 00:00:32,807
What problems we came across,
how we solved them, what the

11
00:00:32,807 --> 00:00:36,217
results of that whole process
were, what did we learn along the

12
00:00:36,217 --> 00:00:38,587
way, and what our next steps are.

13
00:00:40,477 --> 00:00:42,417
But why AI reviews?

14
00:00:43,087 --> 00:00:46,777
We had critical manual findings in
one project in the middle of last

15
00:00:46,777 --> 00:00:51,307
year, and as a result, we needed to
review seventy plus more projects.

16
00:00:51,637 --> 00:00:56,568
The issue was one of those manual
reviews took a person like two or three

17
00:00:56,577 --> 00:01:01,607
days of work, and we simply didn't have
enough budget or people for those manual

18
00:01:01,617 --> 00:01:04,877
reviews So we knew we had to turn to AI.

19
00:01:06,257 --> 00:01:07,907
But why did we build our own tool?

20
00:01:08,607 --> 00:01:10,177
First of all, compliance.

21
00:01:10,227 --> 00:01:13,547
We simply couldn't use a
public API from Anthropic or

22
00:01:13,617 --> 00:01:16,357
OpenAI for that client of ours.

23
00:01:16,877 --> 00:01:20,527
Then we wanted better long-term
cost control, so if one of the model

24
00:01:20,527 --> 00:01:25,477
providers simply decides to jack up the
prices, we can just switch the model

25
00:01:25,487 --> 00:01:27,317
or the model provider or even both.

26
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:30,037
Then we wanted complete
model independence.

27
00:01:30,427 --> 00:01:34,437
Complete meaning we technically
wanted to be able to switch

28
00:01:34,437 --> 00:01:36,047
to any model at any time.

29
00:01:36,397 --> 00:01:39,357
There are obviously quality
differences, and we can't just use

30
00:01:39,377 --> 00:01:41,617
any model, but technically we could.

31
00:01:42,197 --> 00:01:46,297
And then we wanted better control over
the review criteria because, as I said,

32
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:51,477
we had that specific critical finding,
and we needed to check a lot of projects

33
00:01:51,557 --> 00:01:56,297
about this finding, so we needed to make
sure that the review process actually

34
00:01:56,307 --> 00:02:01,327
looks for that specific finding So
we started with our initial approach

35
00:02:01,417 --> 00:02:03,867
being a simple prompt-based approach.

36
00:02:04,787 --> 00:02:06,727
We used OpenCode as an agent runner.

37
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:11,047
OpenCode because we had previous
experience with that and code reviews

38
00:02:11,047 --> 00:02:14,567
are code anyway, so that might just work.

39
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:21,587
So we had one prompt, one detailed
task markdown, writing down exactly

40
00:02:21,617 --> 00:02:25,427
what we wanted the agent to do in
the review, then multiple checklists.

41
00:02:25,427 --> 00:02:32,407
A checklist in that sense being a
cross-project list of review criteria by

42
00:02:32,637 --> 00:02:38,807
project type, topic, or technology stack,
and one report template so the agent knows

43
00:02:38,837 --> 00:02:41,087
what the final report should look like.

44
00:02:42,857 --> 00:02:47,407
And the results look good at first,
because everything that comes

45
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:52,337
out of an LLM looks good at first
Which brings us neatly to our first

46
00:02:52,347 --> 00:02:54,507
set of problems and solutions.

47
00:02:54,987 --> 00:02:58,187
So the prompt-based
approach had some problems.

48
00:02:58,227 --> 00:03:02,087
The agents just wouldn't perform
consistent, complete reviews.

49
00:03:02,537 --> 00:03:06,747
They forgot to read files,
they forgot checklist items.

50
00:03:06,877 --> 00:03:10,797
They just didn't really
follow the report template.

51
00:03:11,227 --> 00:03:15,637
And what we initially thought that
it might be the context window, that

52
00:03:15,637 --> 00:03:17,977
it's just too much context at once.

53
00:03:18,347 --> 00:03:22,487
So we introduced some sub-agents
or instructed open code to

54
00:03:22,487 --> 00:03:28,457
specifically spawn sub-agents for
certain tasks, which did help, but

55
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:30,397
it just didn't solve the problem.

56
00:03:30,787 --> 00:03:36,297
And one of the highlights I always bring
up is a review where the agent afterwards

57
00:03:36,297 --> 00:03:42,097
said, "I had this 700-file project and
read 11 files, and now I give you the

58
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:47,007
whole report over the full project."
Which of course is completely useless

59
00:03:47,007 --> 00:03:52,157
because even the most skilled human or
agent could not give you a full report

60
00:03:52,157 --> 00:03:57,647
for a project by reading only 11 files
of it But those weren't all the issues.

61
00:03:58,017 --> 00:04:02,217
We had a very interesting problem
when we switched from Claude Sonnet

62
00:04:02,217 --> 00:04:07,827
4.5 to 4.6, where the agent suddenly
started actively ignoring large parts

63
00:04:07,827 --> 00:04:12,007
of the repository even though the
instructions clearly said that it is

64
00:04:12,077 --> 00:04:14,467
very important to review everything.

65
00:04:15,137 --> 00:04:18,307
We simply had to face the truth
that trying to force it through

66
00:04:18,307 --> 00:04:22,967
the prompt is simply not reliable,
and we really tried a lot there.

67
00:04:24,407 --> 00:04:28,347
We, of course, also asked the agents
why they did that, so we asked

68
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:33,547
basically for rationalization, which
was not particularly useful, but it

69
00:04:33,557 --> 00:04:37,667
was fun, and that's why I want to
share two of those quotes with you.

70
00:04:38,357 --> 00:04:42,057
First of all, "I understood that
the task was to look at everything,

71
00:04:42,057 --> 00:04:45,537
but decided that the review was good
enough after looking at half of it."

72
00:04:46,577 --> 00:04:50,147
And then, "I consider looking at these
files irrelevant in the context of

73
00:04:50,147 --> 00:04:55,197
the review, even though that clearly
violates the rules." Of course,

74
00:04:55,227 --> 00:04:57,127
those are not the exact quotes.

75
00:04:57,147 --> 00:05:00,917
They are paraphrased slightly because
the LMS produce rationalizations

76
00:05:00,947 --> 00:05:05,617
that are, say, a lot more
wordy than those short quotes.

77
00:05:06,227 --> 00:05:09,537
But either way, in the end, we
knew that with our prompt-based

78
00:05:09,537 --> 00:05:11,157
approach, we were at an end.

79
00:05:11,177 --> 00:05:12,907
We just couldn't continue further.

80
00:05:12,907 --> 00:05:16,107
It was just not reliable
to go down that path.

81
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:22,357
But how do you work with someone
or something that seemingly doesn't

82
00:05:22,357 --> 00:05:27,627
only know the concept of malicious
compliance, but has fully internalized it?

83
00:05:28,637 --> 00:05:33,187
We thought we needed a deterministic
process and some enforcement.

84
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:38,337
First of all, we looked for something
where we could take our review steps

85
00:05:38,348 --> 00:05:43,157
from the task markdown file into a
deterministic review process, and

86
00:05:43,157 --> 00:05:44,977
for that, we found Strends Agents.

87
00:05:45,307 --> 00:05:49,517
Strends Agents SDK is a really nice
way to work with agents, by the way.

88
00:05:51,007 --> 00:05:54,627
Then we took the steps that were
programmatically solvable, something

89
00:05:54,637 --> 00:05:59,527
like reading in the checklist items,
generating the final report from the

90
00:05:59,537 --> 00:06:01,367
review templates, and stuff like that.

91
00:06:03,077 --> 00:06:07,137
Next, we looked at the steps that the
LLMs should solve, and for those, we

92
00:06:07,137 --> 00:06:11,577
gave the agents only the very specific
tools that they needed for that step.

93
00:06:11,997 --> 00:06:16,347
For example, when they are checking if a
checklist is applicable for the project,

94
00:06:16,677 --> 00:06:21,438
they don't have to have access to tools
for issue management and stuff like that.

95
00:06:21,887 --> 00:06:26,437
And also for those steps, we had a
very focused prompt so the agents

96
00:06:26,437 --> 00:06:28,187
know exactly what they should do.

97
00:06:29,717 --> 00:06:34,837
Finally, each step has a validation stage,
and without a successful validation, the

98
00:06:34,837 --> 00:06:36,937
process does not move to the next step.

99
00:06:38,277 --> 00:06:42,127
Most of you are probably thinking right
now, "Wait a minute. That man in the

100
00:06:42,127 --> 00:06:44,547
video, isn't he talking about a harness?"

101
00:06:44,957 --> 00:06:46,827
And yes, I am talking about a harness.

102
00:06:47,197 --> 00:06:50,047
But at the time where we had that
idea, at the end of last year,

103
00:06:50,357 --> 00:06:52,417
that term really wasn't widespread.

104
00:06:54,397 --> 00:06:57,297
The important thing, though,
is did it solve our problems?

105
00:06:57,497 --> 00:06:59,227
And yes, it did, mostly.

106
00:07:00,137 --> 00:07:03,147
By taking programmatic control
over checklists and files,

107
00:07:03,637 --> 00:07:06,977
we reduced hallucinations and
forgetting of tasks dramatically.

108
00:07:07,727 --> 00:07:11,777
We could enforce the completeness by
requiring the individual results to be

109
00:07:11,777 --> 00:07:13,787
handed over to the programmatic process.

110
00:07:14,877 --> 00:07:18,537
And since the report was now generated
programmatically, it did finally

111
00:07:18,537 --> 00:07:20,277
adhere to the templates we set.

112
00:07:20,727 --> 00:07:24,767
And if for some reason a result
was missing, we could just send

113
00:07:24,777 --> 00:07:28,207
the agent back to the necessary
step and make it fix their mistake.

114
00:07:29,677 --> 00:07:32,707
Now this process, of course,
needs some kind of working memory.

115
00:07:32,917 --> 00:07:36,357
So behind the scenes, we went ahead
and carefully designed a memory file

116
00:07:36,367 --> 00:07:41,447
storage, which, for one, serves obviously
as the raw data behind the report,

117
00:07:42,037 --> 00:07:47,577
but also helps us achieve future goals
like resuming interrupted reviews, at

118
00:07:47,577 --> 00:07:52,167
some point maybe incremental reviews,
and, as a bit of foreshadowing, later

119
00:07:52,167 --> 00:07:57,427
processing of the report data Speaking
of the reports, we of course needed to

120
00:07:57,427 --> 00:08:01,997
make the results accessible, and we hit a
roadblock there pretty much immediately.

121
00:08:02,287 --> 00:08:06,807
While during the test runs, we gave
the reports to some people to evaluate

122
00:08:06,817 --> 00:08:11,977
them, and they obviously read the
whole reports in the production stage.

123
00:08:12,617 --> 00:08:15,797
Pretty much no one wanted to
read a large markdown report,

124
00:08:15,867 --> 00:08:17,477
and they were really large.

125
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:22,827
So our first step there was to break
that off into multiple focused report

126
00:08:22,837 --> 00:08:29,007
templates, which helped a bit, but the
LLM output is still very wordy, and so

127
00:08:29,187 --> 00:08:31,427
no one really wanted to read that either.

128
00:08:33,037 --> 00:08:38,277
It dawned on us that markdown and text
are inherently just too difficult to read.

129
00:08:38,857 --> 00:08:44,177
So we slop-coded a visualization tool
and integrated it into our quality

130
00:08:44,177 --> 00:08:47,107
dashboard, which worked perfectly fine.

131
00:08:47,457 --> 00:08:50,597
The developers actually interacted
with the reports, clicked through

132
00:08:50,617 --> 00:08:53,707
the files, through the issues,
through the checklist items.

133
00:08:54,027 --> 00:08:55,077
They could filter them.

134
00:08:55,547 --> 00:08:56,317
They were happy.

135
00:08:56,427 --> 00:09:02,923
They really used that tool and gave
us absolutely positive feedback Riding

136
00:09:02,923 --> 00:09:06,783
on this high, we thought, how could
we make the developers even happier?

137
00:09:07,233 --> 00:09:10,423
And we created an MCP server
for interacting with the report

138
00:09:10,463 --> 00:09:12,463
results right in the IDE.

139
00:09:12,943 --> 00:09:17,393
And that MCP server worked perfectly
fine, but in the end, no one used it.

140
00:09:17,493 --> 00:09:22,093
Okay, I used it, and it really
worked very well, but the developers

141
00:09:22,293 --> 00:09:23,413
just didn't want to use it.

142
00:09:23,723 --> 00:09:24,883
They wanted the report.

143
00:09:24,913 --> 00:09:28,453
They wanted to read it, take the
information out of it that they needed

144
00:09:28,463 --> 00:09:33,243
for their specific task, and then get
along with it and not chat to a, an

145
00:09:33,273 --> 00:09:35,463
LLM to solve the problem for them.

146
00:09:37,123 --> 00:09:40,683
Even with that minor setbacks,
we still have the results that

147
00:09:40,903 --> 00:09:42,473
pretty much speak for themselves.

148
00:09:43,513 --> 00:09:47,143
We started in that whole operation
with two to three days of manual effort

149
00:09:47,703 --> 00:09:52,893
for one review, and we reduced that to
one to two hours plus twenty euros in

150
00:09:52,893 --> 00:09:55,933
tokens, which is an amazing reduction.

151
00:09:57,673 --> 00:10:00,513
Of course, we all know that
LLMs make funny and/or stupid

152
00:10:00,513 --> 00:10:03,733
mistakes, and honestly, that
happens in our process, too.

153
00:10:04,353 --> 00:10:08,113
But we still feel that we are at ninety
percent of the review quality a human

154
00:10:08,113 --> 00:10:10,273
expert would provide in two to three days.

155
00:10:11,073 --> 00:10:14,793
And those ten percent, developers
just ignore them and take the value

156
00:10:14,793 --> 00:10:16,393
out of the remaining ninety percent.

157
00:10:17,753 --> 00:10:21,643
Combined with the efficient result
interaction we built, we got almost

158
00:10:21,643 --> 00:10:23,553
exclusively positive feedback.

159
00:10:24,363 --> 00:10:27,473
And while it was true that when
I submitted that talk, we had

160
00:10:27,473 --> 00:10:31,643
seventy-plus projects, I can happily
say that up until now, we have

161
00:10:31,643 --> 00:10:35,863
created immense value for over four
hundred projects with that process.

162
00:10:36,963 --> 00:10:40,853
But what's probably more important to
you is what did we learn along the way?

163
00:10:41,803 --> 00:10:46,403
First of all, even good prompts are no
guarantee, especially the longer ones.

164
00:10:46,693 --> 00:10:49,933
The longer a prompt is, the
less likely the agent actually

165
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:51,773
follows all of its instructions.

166
00:10:53,053 --> 00:10:56,643
And even if they seemingly do,
LLMs tend to follow instructions

167
00:10:56,673 --> 00:10:57,853
while missing the point.

168
00:10:58,213 --> 00:11:02,603
For example, very early on in the process,
we had a requirement that in the memory

169
00:11:02,613 --> 00:11:07,033
files at the end, every file in the
repository needs to be marked as reviewed.

170
00:11:07,693 --> 00:11:12,463
Then the LLM at some point said those are
a lot of files, and I don't think there's

171
00:11:12,493 --> 00:11:17,603
anything important in there, so I just
take a batch job and mark every file in

172
00:11:17,603 --> 00:11:22,343
a specific category as reviewed without
actually reviewing the file," which

173
00:11:22,343 --> 00:11:24,703
is, of course, fully missing the point.

174
00:11:25,913 --> 00:11:28,783
Combining that with the strange
behavioral change from CloudSonnet

175
00:11:28,793 --> 00:11:33,103
four point five to four point six means
that LLM outputs should be continuously

176
00:11:33,103 --> 00:11:37,693
evaluated if you want to make sure that
your process quality stays the same

177
00:11:37,723 --> 00:11:39,813
or at some point, maybe even improves.

178
00:11:40,763 --> 00:11:43,263
We also think that a harness is essential.

179
00:11:43,683 --> 00:11:47,833
But to be fair, nowadays, this
shouldn't really be news to anyone.

180
00:11:49,421 --> 00:11:53,341
What is still more widespread is to
just throw everything at the LLM.

181
00:11:54,041 --> 00:11:57,311
But if a script can do the job, a
script really should do the job.

182
00:11:57,681 --> 00:12:01,351
Of course, you can write the script
with an LLM, but once the script is

183
00:12:01,351 --> 00:12:05,861
written, it doesn't incur any more cost
for each run, and it can also be tested

184
00:12:05,871 --> 00:12:09,891
deterministically, making it easier
to uphold a certain level of quality.

185
00:12:10,541 --> 00:12:15,461
In the end, both LLMs and scripts are
tools which need to be used properly.

186
00:12:17,251 --> 00:12:20,831
While not being a major learning,
it's probably interesting for you what

187
00:12:20,831 --> 00:12:22,641
kind of model performance we observed.

188
00:12:23,271 --> 00:12:27,421
With the Anthropic models, Haiku
was just not reliable with tool

189
00:12:27,421 --> 00:12:31,071
usage and also has a way too small
context window, so in the end it was

190
00:12:31,111 --> 00:12:33,061
basically unusable for that task.

191
00:12:33,591 --> 00:12:36,701
Sonnet, on the other hand, was
the cost-benefit sweet spot.

192
00:12:37,461 --> 00:12:41,751
Opus, while being slightly better than
Sonnet when looking at the results, was

193
00:12:41,801 --> 00:12:44,291
way slower and ten times more expensive.

194
00:12:45,251 --> 00:12:48,791
And those ten times aren't just a
figure of speech, it's actually ten

195
00:12:48,791 --> 00:12:50,601
times more expensive in our testing.

196
00:12:51,871 --> 00:12:56,571
As for local models, and I'm talking about
my M4 Max MacBook Pro here with thirty-six

197
00:12:56,571 --> 00:13:01,521
gigabytes of unified memory, they do work
in principle, but they are very slow.

198
00:13:02,011 --> 00:13:04,851
And not only that they are slow,
they also have a big quality

199
00:13:04,901 --> 00:13:06,891
bottleneck, the context window size.

200
00:13:08,071 --> 00:13:11,961
So at least with the 27B models I
can run in my machine, the quality

201
00:13:11,961 --> 00:13:16,341
is significantly below Sonnet and
therefore sadly not usable right now.

202
00:13:16,991 --> 00:13:20,181
But with the advancements in
that field and the much beefier

203
00:13:20,271 --> 00:13:24,391
hardware we ordered, that may very
well change in the near future.

204
00:13:25,731 --> 00:13:29,371
Speaking of that near future,
what are our next steps here?

205
00:13:30,121 --> 00:13:34,471
We of course want to evaluate new models,
especially local ones, as they come out.

206
00:13:35,581 --> 00:13:40,131
We also want to try to introduce tools
for deterministic structural analysis,

207
00:13:40,291 --> 00:13:44,931
like Graphify, into the process to
further improve token and time efficiency.

208
00:13:45,761 --> 00:13:49,941
Then, especially when we go into the
incremental reviews direction, we want

209
00:13:49,941 --> 00:13:54,111
to introduce checklist and findings
management so people can properly work

210
00:13:54,141 --> 00:13:56,411
with reviews over a long period of time.

211
00:13:57,381 --> 00:14:01,581
Finally, we may look into creating
pull requests and fixes automatically.

212
00:14:01,741 --> 00:14:04,961
However, currently this is
out of scope for the use cases

213
00:14:04,961 --> 00:14:06,221
we built that process for.

214
00:14:07,171 --> 00:14:10,691
And with that, I want to thank you for
your time and hope the insights from

215
00:14:10,691 --> 00:14:12,731
this presentation were valuable to you.

